In the past few years, there has been strong interest in using Tibased metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for photocatalytic HER and related reactions.19-25 For example, amino-functionalized TiMOFs have been used as light-harvesting antenna to drive photocatalytic HER by encapsulated Pt nanoparticles and molecular Co catalysts.26-28 However, as photosensitizers, TiMOFs are limited by low molar absorptivities, large HOMOLUMO gaps, and relatively short excited state lifetimes. We surmise that synthetic tunability of MOFs can be leveraged to design new Ti-MOFs with much enhanced activities of and for discovering new fundamental insights into photocatalytic HER. Herein we report the synthesis of two new MOFs, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru, by doping $\mathrm{[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]^{+}}$ or $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbpy)}]^{2+}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathsf{b p y}}&{{}=}&{2,2^{\circ}}\end{array}$ -bipyridine, $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{\ppy~}}&{{}=}&{2\cdot}\end{array}$ phenylpyridine) photosensitizers into the $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC $\mathbf{\langleBPDC\rangle}=$ biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) framework for efficient visible lightdriven HER (Figure 1).29,30  Experimental and computational studies reveal the catalytic HER pathway via electron injection into $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{^{IV}(O H)}_{2}$ SBUs to generate $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{III}_{-}O H}}}$ which is protonated and followed by proton-coupled electron transfer to afford the key $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}.$ - $\mathrm{~H~}$ intermediate. Protonation of the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}_{-\mathrm{{H}}}}}}$ species produces hydrogen and regenerates catalytic $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{IV}}{}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBUs.
The photosensitizing bridging ligands $\mathrm{[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]C l(H_{2}L_{1})}$ and $\mathrm{[Ru(bpy)_{2}(d c b p y)]C l_{2}}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathbf L_{2})$ were synthesized according to literature procedures.30,31  Two new MOFs with mixed BPDC and $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ or $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ ligands, $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru, were prepared as golden yellow and deep red rhombic crystals, respectively, by heating $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ $\mathrm{\hat{a}b}z=4$ -aminobenzoate), $^{32}\mathrm{~H_{2}B P D C}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}$ in $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF） at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days with acetic acid (AcOH) as modulator (Figure 2a).33 Optical and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) imaging showed that both $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru adopted the same rhombic morphology as $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC (Figure 2b, c and Figure S5, S6), whereas powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) studies revealed that they were isostructural to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC (Figure 2d). The porous structures of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru were demonstrated by type I nitrogen sorption isotherms with Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of 325 and $375\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively (Figure 2e). Inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis of digested $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru showed a Ti/Ir ratio of 3.25 and $\mathrm{Ti/Ru}$ ratio of 4.82, respectively. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of $\mathrm{D}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ -digested $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru gave a $\mathrm{BPDC/L_{1}}$ ratio of 2.0 and a $\mathrm{BPDC}/\mathbf{L}_{2}$ of 4.06 (Figure S7, S8), respectively. These results correspond to a Ti/Ir ratio of 3.0 for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and a Ti/Ru ration of 5.0 for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru. The empirical formulae of $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir  and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru are thus  approximated  as $\mathrm{[Ti_{3}(O H)_{2}(B P D C)_{2}(L_{1})](O A c)_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{\Gamma[Ti_{3}(O H)_{2}(B P D C)_{2.4}}.$ $({\bf L}_{2})_{0.6}](\mathrm{OAc})_{4}$ ,respectively. These formulae were supported by thermogravimetric analysis results which showed residual weights of $28.3\%$ (expected $27.8\%$ ）and $22.5\%$ (expected $22.4\%$ ）for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ - BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru, respectively (Figure S9, S10).
Visible light-driven $\lambda>400\mathrm{nm}$ ) HER activities of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru were studied in an $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -free acetonitrile (MeCN)
solution with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the proton source and 1,3-dimethyl-2-phenyl 2,3-dihydro-lH-benzo[d]imidazole (BIH) as the sacrificial agent $\mathrm{(MeCN;H_{2}O}=20;0.5\ \mathrm{V}/\mathrm{V})$ . The amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generated was quantified by gas chromatography (GC) analysis of the headspace gas in the reactor. The turnover numbers (TONs) [defined as $\mathrm{n}(1/2\mathrm{H}_{2})]$ reached 6632 for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and 786 for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDCRu after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ of visible light irradiation, with apparent quantum yields of $0.080\%$ and $0.018\%$ , respectively (Figure 3a). After photocatalytic HER, both $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru maintained their PXRD patterns (Figure 3b) with $<1\%$ leaching of Ti into the solution, indicating their structural stability under reaction conditions. In comparison, the homogeneous mixtures of $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ and photosensitizing ligands $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ or $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ exhibited modest TONs of 80 and 13, respectively. The significant enhancement of photocatalytic HER activities of the MOFs over their homogeneous controls demonstrated the important role of hierarchical assembly of $\mathbf{L}_{1}$ or $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ photosensitizers and catalytic $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBUs in facilitating multi-electron transfer to drive photocatalytic HER.
We next examined whether the excited photosensitizer was reductively quenched by BIH or oxidatively quenched by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBUs to initiate photocatalytic HER. Luminescence spectroscopy showed that the emissions of both $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru could be efficiently quenched by BIH in MeCN (Figure S13). To quantitatively assess the quenching process, we determined luminescence intensities of homogeneous photosensitizers $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}$ in the presence of BIH and $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ in MeCN As shown in Figure 4a, b and Figure Sl4, the luminescence of $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}{\bf L}_{2}$ was efficiently quenched by BIH but not $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ cluster. The luminescence quenching of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}{\bf L}_{1}$ and ${\bf{M e}}_{2}{\bf{L}}_{2}$ by BIH was fitted to the Stern-Volmer equation to afford $K_{s\nu}$ values of $3.36\ \mathrm{\mM^{-1}}$ and $0.15\mathrm{\mM^{-1}}$ ， respectively, indicating more efficient quenching for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir (Figure 4c). These results suggest that photocatalytic HER in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir initiates via electron transfer from BIH to the excited $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ or $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ , and the reduced $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ or $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ transfer electron to the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBU to drive HER.
Cyclic voltammograms (CVs) of $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{Ti}_{3}}$ 一 BPDC were then studied to provide additional insight into the HER process. With the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{H}^{+}$ $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{6}(\mathrm{abz})_{6}$ showed an irreversible reduction peak with an onset potential of -0.17 V(Figure 4d). A similar CV feature was also observed for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ 一 BPDC, suggesting that the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{\Omega}}}}}$ center in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ was first reduced to $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ before the HER. Electron paramagnetic spectroscopy (EPR) confirmed the existence of $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ centers upon visible light irradiation of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru in the absence of a proton source. Upon irradiation, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru exhibited an additional peak with the $\mathbf{g}$ -value of 1.983 and 1.985, respectively, over the homogeneous $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ and ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}$ ligands (Figure 4e and 4f). These signals are attributed to the $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ species.26.34 X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy (XANES) of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru further confirmed the generation of $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ during the photocatalytic process when compared to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC (Figure S11). The Ti pre-edge peak of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru after photoirradiation shifted $0.58~\mathrm{eV}$ to the lower energy compared to those of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ - BPDC-Ru before photoirradiation  and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDCafter photoirradiation. The filling of one electron into the d orbitals lowers their energy and thus decreases the energy of the ${1s\to3\mathrm{d}}$ transition.35
CV scans showed the HER catalytic peak of $\operatorname{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC with an onset potential of $-0.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs NHE in a MeCN solution with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{MeCN}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{TFA}=20{\cdot}0.25{\cdot}0.25,}\end{array}$ V/V/V). The proton concentration of this solution is $10^{7.65}$ higher than that of photocatalytic HER condition $\mathrm{(MeCN;H_{2}O}=20;0.5,$ V/V). The energy needed to drive the HER by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBUs in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir or $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru under the photocatalytic conditions is estimated to be $0.63\mathrm{eV}$ $(\Delta E^{\prime})$ based on the Nernst equation. The corresponding potential energy diagrams are shown in Figure S19.
Based on the well-defined structure of catalytic centers in MOFs. we further propose photocatalytic HER via a $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ -hydride intermediate (Scheme 1b). The $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{{IV}}}}}}$ -OH site first accepts one electron from the reduced photosensitizer $[\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbpy)}]$ to form the Til-OH species. The electron-rich $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ center makes the hydroxyl group basic which can be protonated by water to generate a vacant coordination site on the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{III}}}$ center $\mathrm{[pK_{b}(T i^{\mathrm{III}}-O H)=4.14}$ VS. $\mathrm{pK_{\mathrm{6}}(T i^{\mathrm{1V}}\mathrm{-OH})~=~12.68]}$ . The low-coordinate $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{III}}}$ center undergoes proton coupled electron transfer (PCET) by accepting the proton from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the electron from another reduced photosensitizer $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{ppy})_{2}(\mathrm{dcbpy})]$ to form the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ -hydride intermediate. Protonation of $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ -hydride produces hydrogen and regenerates the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{\scriptscriptstyleiV}}}}.$ -OH catalyst.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculation using the B3LYP functional supported the proposed HER mechanism as shown in Scheme lb. Figure 5 shows energy change along the reaction coordinate of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ir catalyzed HER. Energy from Ir-based photosensitizers $(0.72\mathrm{eV})$ was already taken into account in Figure 5. With the energy and electron from reduced photosensitizer $[\mathrm{{Ir}(p p y)_{2}(d c b p y)}]$ ，the $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{N}}}\mathrm{-OH}}$ center in the MOF is readily reduced to $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}.$ -OH. The dissociation of the OH group from the $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ center is uphill by $0.32~\mathrm{eV}$ , leading to the generation of the $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ center with an open coordination site. Subsequent formation of $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{v}}}$ -hydride via a PCET process is the rate determining step with an energy barrier of $0.76~\mathrm{eV}$ . Electron transfer from the reduced photosensitizer $[\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbpy)}]$ provides the energy to overcome the barrier. The energetically downhill (by $1.24~\mathrm{eV}$ ）hydrogen formation step provides the driving force for the overall reaction. The total energy change in this cycle is $\Delta E_{c a l}=-0.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ ，matching well with our experimental result $(\Delta E_{e x p}=0.18\mathrm{eV})$ .
In summary, we have synthesized two new Ti MOFs by doping $\mathrm{[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]C l-}$ or $\mathrm{[Ru(bpy)_{2}(d c b p y)]C l_{2}.}$ derived dicarboxylate ligands into the Ti-BPDC framework and demonstrated their applications in visible light-driven hydrogen evolution. The proximity of multiple photosensitizers to the catalytic $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBU facilitates multielectron transfer upon photoexcitation, leading to 80-fold enhancement in HER activity over the corresponding homogeneous controls. Photophysical. electrochemical, and DFT studies established the reductive quenching of the photoexcited photosensitizers by BIH as the initiating step of photocatalytic HER followed by electron transfer from the reduced photosensitizers to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ SBUs. Protonation of $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{-OH}}$ generates the $\operatorname{Ti}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ species with a vacant coordination site which undergoes proton-coupled electron transfer to afford the key $\mathrm{Ti^{\mathrm{{IV}}}\mathrm{-\mathrm{{H}}}}$ intermediate. This work thus provides new insights into the design of multifunctional Ti MOFs with catalytic SBUs and photosensitizing ligands for photocatalytic applications.
